Give Aerium a Media section in Settings, and make DRM an opt-in switch in it.

Aerium does not ship Google's CDM, so it should not tell sites it has one until
somebody asks - the arrangement Brave uses. That switch now lives in a Media
section of its own, between Appearance and Search engine in the sidebar, rather
than tacked onto a page it does not belong to. Playback settings had nowhere to
live; this is where the rest of them go.

Why a command-line switch, and why read at that exact moment. This replaces a
chrome://flags entry that was removed for not working, and that failure decides
the shape of this one. On Linux the CDM is loaded before the zygote is
sandboxed, so registration runs from PreSandboxInit, long before chrome://flags
entries become switches in the browser process; a SINGLE_VALUE_TYPE flag could
never be visible when the decision was made. The window that IS still open is
the browser process's own registration: BrowserMainLoop::PreCreateThreads()
calls parts_->PreCreateThreads() and then CdmRegistry::Init(), in that order,
and CdmRegistry::Init() is what calls AddContentDecryptionModules() for the
browser. So the pref is read at the end of
ChromeBrowserMainParts::PreCreateThreads(), by which point PreCreateThreadsImpl
has loaded Local State, and turned into the switch cdm_registration.cc reads a
moment later.

Stated plainly rather than implied: on Linux the zygote has already dlopen'd
libwidevinecdm.so before the sandbox closed and nothing here can undo that. The
switch does not claim to. What it controls is whether the browser REGISTERS the
CDM, which is what decides whether any page can request Widevine. Off means no
site can use it. On Windows registration is component-updater driven and happens
later, so the same gate covers loading as well as use.

Local State rather than a profile pref, because PreCreateThreads runs before any
profile exists - the whole reason the timing works. settings_private reaches it
regardless: FindServiceForPref() falls through to local_state() for any pref the
profile does not have, so the toggle binds like any other and the page composes
PrefsMixin normally.

Read via a switch rather than directly, because cdm_registration.cc lives in
chrome/common and has no business reaching into the browser process for a
PrefService. It also means every platform reads one thing - Android sets the
same switch from Java before native starts, early enough to gate registration
outright.

The section is modelled on the Aerium Guard page, which is the working example
in this series of adding a settings section: route, router, settings_main view
slot and import, settings_menu entry, BUILD.gn web component. It is not behind a
pageVisibility flag, for the same reason Guard is not.

Three hunks - BUILD.gn, router.ts and settings_main.ts - carry lines added by
aerium-guard-settings-section.patch as context, because that patch is earlier in
the series and touches the same regions. Their presence is required for this
patch to apply, which is correct: series order guarantees it.

Generated by applying the change to real files at the 152.0.7977.75 tag and
diffing, not written by hand. Verified with git apply --check and patch -p1
against a tree carrying the Guard patch's lines, and every anchor confirmed to
match exactly once.
